17:["$","main",null,{"id":"top","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-ink/10 bg-surface/40","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto max-w-5xl px-5 py-10 sm:px-8","children":[["$","$Ld",null,{"href":"/operating-system","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-semibold text-mute transition hover:text-accent","children":"← All chapters"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-5 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm font-semibold text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-accent/12 px-2.5 py-0.5","children":"Chapter 27"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-mute","children":["· ",17," min read · illustrated"]}]]}],["$","h1",null,{"className":"mt-3 font-hand text-5xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-6xl","children":"Disks, SSDs & Disk Scheduling"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-lg text-mute","children":"The slowest thing your program touches, and every trick the OS uses to make it hurt less"}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mt-6 flex max-w-2xl flex-col gap-3","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"For most of this course the machine has moved at the speed of the CPU and RAM, nanoseconds, billions of operations a second. Storage is where that speed collapses. The moment your data has to survive a power cut, it lands on a device that is thousands to millions of times slower than the memory the CPU is used to, and everything about how the OS behaves near that device is shaped by trying to hide the gap. This chapter is about the device at the bottom of the memory hierarchy: how a spinning disk physically works, how a solid-state drive fakes being one, and how the OS decides the order in which pending requests are served."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"We will build the cost model of a hard disk from its moving parts, platters, a spinning surface, a seeking arm, and see exactly why a thousand scattered reads can be a hundred times slower than one big sequential read of the same bytes. Then we will watch the OS reorder a queue of pending requests with the classic disk-scheduling algorithms, working the arithmetic by hand so the trade-offs are concrete rather than named. Finally we cross over to flash: no moving parts, but a strange new rule, you cannot overwrite in place, you must erase first, that forces a whole hidden translation layer inside the drive."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/85","children":"The payoff is not trivia. Sequential-versus-random is the single most important performance fact in storage, and it still dictates how databases lay out their files, why write-ahead logs and log-structured designs win, and what you are actually looking at when `iostat` says a disk is at 100% utilisation. This is the hardware reality that the file systems in the next chapters are built on top of."}]]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"mx-auto flex max-w-6xl gap-10 px-5 py-12 sm:px-8","children":[["$","nav",null,{"className":"sticky top-8 hidden h-fit w-56 shrink-0 lg:block","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 font-hand text-xl font-bold text-ink","children":"On this page"}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-1.5 text-sm","children":[["$","li","01-storage-is-slow",{"children":["$","a",null,{"href":"#01-storage-is-slow","className":"flex gap-2 rounded-md px-2 py-1 text-mute transition hover:bg-accent/10 hover:text-accent","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"text-accent/70","children":"01"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"leading-snug","children":"Why storage is the slow part"}]]}]}],"$L21","$L22","$L23","$L24","$L25","$L26","$L27"]}]]}],"$L28"]}],"$L29"]}]
